ne row. Fine.\n\n12:\nSELECT MAX(f.`Free Meal Count (Ages 5-17)` * 1.0 / f.`Enrollment (Ages 5-17)`)\nFROM frpm f JOIN satscores t ON f.CDSCode = t.cds\nWHERE t.NumGE1500 * 1.0 / t.NumTstTakr > 0.3\n\nType real. Good.\n\n23:\nSELECT s.School, s.Street\nFROM schools s JOIN frpm f ON s.CDSCode = f.CDSCode\nWHERE f.`Enrollment (K-12)` - f.`Enrollment (Ages 5-17)` > 30\n\n24:\nSELECT f.`School Name`\nFROM frpm f JOIN satscores t ON f.CDSCode = t.cds\nWHERE f.`Free Meal Count (K-12)` / f.`Enrollment (K-12)` > 0.1\nAND t.NumGE1500 > 0\n\nSQLite division of REAL columns yields real because declared REAL and values floats. Good. To be safe multiply 1.0. But if integer values? Declared REAL; SQLite dynamic typing could store ints? sample floats. Even if ints, `/` gives integer division if both ints. Better use *1.0 for ratio to ensure correct. Gold uses maybe just division. But result set values could differ due to integer division only if both are integers. Let's check types: enrollment stored REAL? sample 1087.0. likely all float. But use *1.0 robust. For q24 threshold 0.1, integer division would truncate and alter drastically. Use *1.0.\n\n25:\nSELECT t.sname, f.`Charter Funding Type`\nFROM satscores t JOIN frpm f ON t.cds = f.CDSCode\nWHERE f.`District Name` LIKE 'Riverside%' AND t.AvgScrMath > 400\n\n26:\nSELECT f.`School Name`, s.Street, s.City, s.State, s.Zip\nFROM frpm f JOIN schools s ON f.CDSCode = s.CDSCode\nWHERE f.`County Name` = 'Monterey'\nAND f.`FRPM Count (Ages 5-17)` > 800\nAND f.`School Type` = 'High Schools (Public)'\n\n27:\nSELECT s.School, t.AvgScrWrite, s.Phone\nFROM schools s LEFT JOIN satscores t ON s.CDSCode = t.cds\nWHERE s.OpenDate > '1991-12-31' OR s.ClosedDate < '2000-01-01'\n\n31:\nSELECT f.`Free Meal Count (K-12)` * 1.0 / f.`Enrollment (K-12)`\nFROM frpm f\nORDER BY f.`Enrollment (K-12)` DESC\nLIMIT 2 OFFSET 9\n\nPotential gold uses qualification no OFFSET? Should be correct.\n\n5:\nSELECT COUNT(*)\nFROM satscores t JOIN schools s ON t.cds = s